#import "/_lib/0.1.1.typ": *
#show: template

#taxon[Lemma]
#title[Unit Law Dirichlet Product]

Define $I : "FinSet" -> "Set"$ by 
$
  I(S) :-= (S ~= bb(1)_"FinSet")
$

Note that $(S ~= bb(1)_"FinSet")$ is a proposition (TODO: explain) so in particular a set.

Note also that this fits the definition of $I$ in the #local("00DU")[paper] quite well.

We prove that
$
  F *_D ~= F,
$
by constructing for each $S : "FinSet"$ a map
$
  f : (F *_D I)(S) -> F(S)
$
and showing it has contractible fibers.

#proof[
  A term of type $(F *_D I) (S)$ is of the form
  $
    (T,U,e,a,b)
  $
  where
  $
    T,U: "FinSet", .. e: T xx U ~= S, .. a : F(T), .. b: I(U).
  $
  Note that $b: U ~= bb(1)$, so $U$ contractible.
  We denote its center of contraction by $y_0 :U$.
  We now define an equivalence
  $
  p : T ~= S,
  $
  by $p(t) :-= e(t,y_0)$. Because $U$ is contractible, $T xx U ~= T$, so $p$ is indeed an equivalence.
  
  By univalence $p$ gives us a path
  $
    r : T = S.
  $
  
  Define
  $
    f(T,U,e,a,b) :-= "tr"_F (r,a) : F(S)
  $
  
  Now we show that the fibers of $f$ are contracible:
  
  Let $a_0: F(S)$. Then the fiber is given by
  $
    "fib"_f (a_0) -= sum (x : (F *_D I)(S)), f(x) = a_0. 
  $
  
  
  Define
  $
   x_0 :-= (S, bb(1), e_0, a_0, b_0),
  $
  where $e_0 : S xx bb(1) ~= S$ is the right unit equivalence and $b_0 : bb(1) ~= bb(1)$ is the identity.
  
  Then
  $
    f(x_0) = "tr"_F("refl"_S, a_0) = a_0.
  $
  
  Pick $(x_0, "refl"_(a_0))$ as the center.
  
  We now show that given $(x,q) : "fib"_f (a_0)$, with
  $
    x -= (T,U,e,a,b), .. q : f(s) = a_0,
  $
  $(x_0, "refl"_(a_0)) = (x,q)$ holds.
  
  Observe, that using $r : T = S$ (from $p : T~=S$), we get that
  $
    f(s) = "tr"_F (r,a)
  $.
  
  this means we get an element
  $
    q : "tr"_F (r,a) = a_0
  $
  
  (that we also call $q$ by abuse of notation)
  
  We now use the the #local("00E4")[characterization of the Identity type of Dirichlet Product], so we only need to give a path
  $
    (T,U,e,a,b) = (S,bb(1),e_0, a_0, b_0)
  $
  
  Thus we need
  $
    r : T = S,\
    s: U =1,\
    h : e_0 @ (r xx s) = e,\
    g : "tr"_F (r,a) = a_0,\
    k : "tr"_I (s,b) = b_0,\
  $
  We already have have $r$. Define $s$ by applying univalence to $b$. Set $g :-= q$.
  Note that $"tr"_I(s,b) : I(1)$, which is contracible,so
  $
    "tr"_I (s,b) = b_0.
  $
  
  It only reamains to construct $h$.
  (TODO: copy from Notebook II)
]
